matically to add those files. \n\n`datalad add` and `datalad save` will typically add the files to annex, but here are some cases where they'll add files to git directly:\n\n  * They are called with the `--to-git` flag.\n\n  * git-annex is configured to store the content directly in git (more on that below).\n\n  * The repository isn't an annex repository.  This will happen if you call `datalad {add,save}` in a repository that was created with `git init` and `git annex init` has *not* been called.  If you're creating the repository with `datalad create`, you don't have to worry about this because by default it will create an annex repo (i.e, it will call `git annex init` for you).\n\n\u003e Maybe this can be implemented if the file content is higher than a certain threshold.\n\nContinuing from the second point above, git-annex [has a method][1] for configuring which files go where.  You can use this to implement the cutoff.  To use the example at that page, a `.gitattributes` file with\n\n```\n* annex.largefiles=(largerthan=100kb)\n```\n\nwould mean to put files larger than 100kb into annex.\n\nDataLad can configure one common case for you: track text files with git and put everything else into the annex.  You can run `datalad run-procedure cfg_text2git` in an existing annex repo (or use `datalad create -c text2git` when creating a new repo).  That will add the following line to the .gitattributes file:\n\n```\n* annex.largefiles=(not(mimetype=text/*))\n```\n\n[1]: https://git-annex.branchable.com/tips/largefiles/","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"5f28eb6cf9dc13eefe95e9c4af8c2adace24728a"},"timestamp":1567780630,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUyODg4MDQyNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3632#issuecomment-528880427"},"message":"Hi,\n\nThank you for your message - This is why I got confused as I used a\n*datalad create *\n\nI tried adding a file to annex with  *git annex add file* and I looks like\nit adds the file but gives this error:\n\n\n\n\n\n*add 2015-11-18-6-9electrodein-iso0.5.tif ok\n (recording state in git...)fatal: in unpopulated submodule\n'projects/FRDR-multimodal'git-annex: user error (xargs\n[\"-0\",\"git\",\"--git-dir=../../../../.git\",\"--work-tree=../../../..\",\"--literal-pathspecs\",\"add\",\"--\"]\nexited 123)*\n\nFiles seem to be tracked by annex and I would now want to clean it. This\nbrings a question:\n\nWhat is the difference between all these? If I clean my repository now,\nkind of an un-annex operation, am I supposed to see the both back to 0?\n\n1) local annex keys\n2) annexed files in working tree\n\nThanks a lot!!\n\nGiulia\n\n\n\nIl giorno gio 5 set 2019 alle ore 18:10 Kyle Meyer \u003cnotifications@github.com\u003e\nha scritto:\n\n\u003e I had the suspect it was something like this when I saw annex size 0 bytes\n\u003e but I wonder, I thought github would accept ~50GiB\n\u003e\n\u003e I'm not clear on all the details of their size restrictions, but I do\n\u003e think it is much less than that. Here's a page\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.community/t5/Support-Protips/Working-with-large-files-and-repositories/ba-p/9343\u003e\n\u003e that mentions a 2GB push limit, which is consistent with what you're seeing.\n\u003e\n\u003e and I was sure that datalad commands would trigger git-annex automatically\n\u003e to add those files.\n\u003e\n\u003e datalad add and datalad save will typically add the files to annex, but\n\u003e here are some cases where they'll add files to git directly:\n\u003e\n\u003e    -\n\u003e\n\u003e    They are called with the --to-git flag.\n\u003e    -\n\u003e\n\u003e    git-annex is configured to store the content directly in git (more on\n\u003e    that below).\n\u003e    -\n\u003e\n\u003e    The repository isn't an annex repository. This will happen if you call datalad\n\u003e    {add,save} in a repository that was created with git init and git\n\u003e    annex init has *not* been called. If you're creating the repository\n\u003e    with datalad create, you don't have to worry about this because by\n\u003e    default it will create an annex repo (i.e, it will call git annex init\n\u003e    for you).\n\u003e\n\u003e Maybe this can be implemented if the file content is higher than a certain\n\u003e threshold.\n\u003e\n\u003e Continuing from the second point above, git-annex has a way\n\u003e \u003chttps://git-annex.branchable.com/tips/largefiles/\u003e for configuring which\n\u003e files go where. You can use this to implement the cutoff. To use the\n\u003e example at that page, a .gitattributes file with\n\u003e\n\u003e * annex.largefiles=(largerthan=100kb)\n\u003e\n\u003e would mean to put files larger than 100kb into annex.\n\u003e\n\u003e DataLad can configure .gitattributes one common case for you: track text\n\u003e files with git and put everything else into the annex. You can run datalad\n\u003e run-procedure cfg_text2git in an existing annex repo (or use datalad\n\u003e create -c text2git when creating a new repo). That will add the following\n\u003e line to the .gitattributes file:\n\u003e\n\u003e * annex.largefiles=(not(mimetype=text/*))\n\u003e\n\u003e —\n\u003e You are receiving this because you authored the thread.\n\u003e Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3632?email_source=notifications\u0026email_token=AEGS2Z2FLMOKLYR526BW6JLQIF7VVA5CNFSM4ISQ2KZ2YY3PNVWWK3TUL52HS4DFVREXG43VMVBW63LNMVXHJKTDN5WW2ZLOORPWSZGOD6A7JIQ#issuecomment-528610466\u003e,\n\u003e or mute the thread\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AEGS2ZZKRZ4WQDVZXCIXZ63QIF7VVANCNFSM4ISQ2KZQ\u003e\n\u003e .\n\u003e","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1567783797,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUyODkwMTMxNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3632#issuecomment-528901317"},"message":"\u003e I tried adding a file to annex with  *git annex add file* and I looks like\n\u003e it adds the file but gives this error:\n\u003e\n\u003e *add 2015-11-18-6-9electrodein-iso0.5.tif ok\n\u003e  (recording state in git...)fatal: in unpopulated submodule\n\u003e 'projects/FRDR-multimodal'git-annex: user error (xargs\n\u003e [\"-0\",\"git\",\"--git-dir=../../../../.git\",\"--work-tree=../../../..\",\"--literal-pathspecs\",\"add\",\"--\"]\n\u003e exited 123)*\n\nThe \"fatal: in unpopulated submodule\" is worrying, but it's hard to\ntroubleshoot without being able to reproduce the issue.  (Quickly making\nan unpopulated submodule, I'm unable to trigger it.)  If you can share a\nreliable and minimal recipe for triggering that error, please post it as\na separate issue.  In that issue, it'd be helpful to provide information\non your OS and versions of git, git-annex, and datalad.\n\n\u003e Files seem to be tracked by annex and I would now want to clean it.\n\nI'm guessing for cleaning you're talking about removing the large files\nas git objects.  Even if you move those files over to annex now, they\nare still present in the repo's history as git objects, so it won't\nhelp.  As I mentioned above, the easiest way to get your repo down to a\nsize GitHub will take is to start a new repo.\n\n\u003e This brings a question:\n\u003e\n\u003e What is the difference between all these?  If I clean my repository\n\u003e now, kind of an un-annex operation, am I supposed to see the both back\n\u003e to 0?\n\u003e\n\u003e 1) local annex keys\n\u003e 2) annexed files in working tree\n\n\"local annex keys\" reports the number of keys you have in\n.git/annex/objects.\n\n\"annexed files in working tree\" refers to the number of annex files in\nthe Git working tree.  In the typical case, this is the number of\nsymlinks to .git/annex/objects that are present in your current branch\nplus/minus any uncommitted changes you've made that add/remove annex\nfiles.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"5f28eb6cf9dc13eefe95e9c4af8c2adace24728a"},"timestamp":1568298143,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzMDg0OTI2MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3632#issuecomment-530849260"},"message":"Hi Kyle,\n\nThank you for your message and apologies for the late reply. I work on\nNeuroHub/conp two days a week only and I left early last time.\n\nSo the \"unpopulated submodule\" eventually disappeared when I set the **\nannex.largefiles=(largerthan=100kb)*\n\nThe submodule was empty, just created with *datalad create*, hence git\nannex initiated. Adding files via *git annex add file* triggered the error\n(I think the right approach here is to use datalad). I will try to\nreplicate it in a different repo once the dataset repository I am working\non is up and available. I will write an issue if necessary.\n\nSo once the *annex.largefiles* was set, a simple *datalad add ** would add\neverything in the right place. So everything went well.\n\nI added one sibling as well and published the dataset to the sibling\nwith *datalad\npublish --to github*\n\nAt this point, when I went to test my dataset, installed it in datalad and\ntried to access annexed files, I noticed something:\n\nThis is the test:\n\n\n*datalad install -r https://github.com/gi114/conp-dataset\n\u003chttps://github.com/gi114/conp-dataset\u003ecd\nconp-dataset/projects/\u003cnew_dataset\u003e*\n\n*datalad get \u003cannexed-file\u003e*\n\nThis generates this error at the get operation:\n```\n[WARNING] Running get resulted in stderr output: git-annex: get: 1 failed\n\n[ERROR  ] not available; Try making some of these repositories available:;\na45566d0-9023-421d-9707-2732be3b3d84 --\nid@host:~/path/to/conp-dataset/projects/FRDR-multimodal;\n; (Note that these git remotes have annex-ignore set: origin)\n[get(/path/conp-dataset/projects/FRDR-multimodal/\u003cannexed-file\u003e)]\nget(error): /path/conp-dataset/projects/FRDR-multimodal/\u003cannexed-file\u003e\n(file) [not available; Try making some of these repositories available:;\na45566d0-9023-421d-9707-2732be3b3d84 --\nid@host:~/path/to/conp-dataset/projects/FRDR-multimodal;\n; (Note that these git remotes have annex-ignore set: origin)]\n```\n\nSo I cannot download the annexed-file content. I searched for the\nannex-ignore variable mentioned in 'Note that these git remotes have\nannex-ignore set: origin' and found annex-ignore in my repository in the\n.git/config and set it to *false* ! Now here the thing. To do this I went\nback to the repository I was working on to set up the whole dataset so when\nI go to download and install the dataset as test to see if everything is\nok  expect to see the change and being able to download annexed files\ncontent. But it occurs that, after setting the variable to false and double\nchecking it, any operation (state change) on the dataset would cause the\nvariable to be set back to its original value, which was *true*. It looks\nlike any operation on the dataset, from creating a new sibling, updating it\nand so on would cause the .git/config file to \"refresh\" to default. Hence\nthe annex-ignore = *true* again. This is a block for actually using the\ndataset.\n\nWhat are the dependencies that this file has? Is there a way to enforce the\nvariable to be set to false as a default instead of true?\n\nLet me know,\n\nBest\nGiulia\n\nIl giorno ven 6 set 2019 alle ore 11:29 Kyle Meyer \u003cnotifications@github.com\u003e\nha scritto:\n\n\u003e \u003e I tried adding a file to annex with *git annex add file* and I looks like\n\u003e \u003e it adds the file but gives this error:\n\u003e \u003e\n\u003e \u003e *add 2015-11-18-6-9electrodein-iso0.5.tif ok\n\u003e \u003e (recording state in git...)fatal: in unpopulated submodule\n\u003e \u003e 'projects/FRDR-multimodal'git-annex: user error (xargs\n\u003e \u003e\n\u003e [\"-0\",\"git\",\"--git-dir=../../../../.git\",\"--work-tree=../../../..\",\"--literal-pathspecs\",\"add\",\"--\"]\n\u003e \u003e exited 123)*\n\u003e\n\u003e The \"fatal: in unpopulated submodule\" is worrying, but it's hard to\n\u003e troubleshoot without being able to reproduce the issue. (Quickly making\n\u003e an unpopulated submodule, I'm unable to trigger it.) If you can share a\n\u003e reliable and minimal recipe for triggering that error, please post it as\n\u003e a separate issue. In that issue, it'd be helpful to provide information\n\u003e on your OS and versions of git, git-annex, and datalad.\n\u003e\n\u003e \u003e Files seem to be tracked by annex and I would now want to clean it.\n\u003e\n\u003e I'm guessing for cleaning you're talking about removing the large files\n\u003e as git objects. Even if you move those files over to annex now, they\n\u003e are still present in the repo's history as git objects, so it won't\n\u003e help. As I mentioned above, the easiest way to get your repo down to a\n\u003e size GitHub will take is to start a new repo.\n\u003e\n\u003e \u003e This brings a question:\n\u003e \u003e\n\u003e \u003e What is the difference between all these? If I clean my repository\n\u003e \u003e now, kind of an un-annex operation, am I supposed to see the both back\n\u003e \u003e to 0?\n\u003e \u003e\n\u003e \u003e 1) local annex keys\n\u003e \u003e 2) annexed files in working tree\n\u003e\n\u003e \"local annex keys\" reports the number of keys you have in\n\u003e .git/annex/objects.\n\u003e\n\u003e \"annexed files in working tree\" refers to the number of annex files in\n\u003e the Git working tree. In the typical case, this is the number of\n\u003e symlinks to .git/annex/objects that are present in your current branch\n\u003e plus/minus any uncommitted changes you've made that add/remove annex\n\u003e files.\n\u003e\n\u003e —\n\u003e You are receiving this because you authored the thread.\n\u003e Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3632?email_source=notifications\u0026email_token=AEGS2ZZLLVGP2L4CN7JD2ETQIJZPPA5CNFSM4ISQ2KZ2YY3PNVWWK3TUL52HS4DFVREXG43VMVBW63LNMVXHJKTDN5WW2ZLOORPWSZGOD6DGJRI#issuecomment-528901317\u003e,\n\u003e or mute the thread\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AEGS2Z3BQ2OOOLDOAMMGYHTQIJZPPANCNFSM4ISQ2KZQ\u003e\n\u003e .\n\u003e","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1568300671,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzMDg2NzcwNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3632#issuecomment-530867705"},"message":"\u003e datalad install -r https://github.com/gi114/conp-dataset\n\u003e *datalad get \u003cannexed-file\u003e* This generates this error at the get operation:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e [WARNING] Running get resulted in stderr output: git-annex: get: 1 failed [ERROR ] not available\n\u003e Try making some of these repositories available:\n\u003e a45566d0-9023-421d-9707-2732be3b3d84 -- id@host:~/path/to/conp-dataset/projects/FRDR-multimodal\n\u003e\n\u003e (Note that these git remotes have annex-ignore set: origin) \n\u003e [...]  \n\u003e ```\n\u003e\n\u003e So I cannot download the annexed-file content. I searched for the annex-ignore variable mentioned in 'Note that these git remotes have annex-ignore set: origin' and found annex-ignore in my repository in the .git/config and set it to *false* !\n\u003e [...]\n\u003e Is there a way to enforce the variable to be set to false as a default instead of true?\n\nHere is the relevant bit from `man git-annex`:\n\n```\n* `remote.\u003cname\u003e.annex-ignore`\n\n  If set to `true`, prevents git-annex\n  from storing file contents on this remote by default.\n  (You can still request it be used by the `--from` and `--to` options.)\n\n  This is, for example, useful if the remote is located somewhere\n  without git-annex-shell. (For example, if it's on GitHub).\n  Or, it could be used if the network connection between two\n  repositories is too slow to be used normally.\n\n  This does not prevent git-annex sync (or the git-annex assistant) from\n  syncing the git repository to the remote.\n```\n\nThis should give you a pretty good idea of what's going on.  GitHub (\"origin\" here) doesn't support storing annex data and doesn't have git-annex-shell available.  git-annex detects this and sets `.annex-ignore` to \"true\".  You can manually unset it, but it will detect again that it can't work with it and set it again to \"true\".\n\nYou can store your Git repository on GitHub, but you need to expose the content of annexed files through some other remote.\n\nHere's some more information:\n\n  * https://git-annex.branchable.com/tips/centralized_git_repository_tutorial/on_GitHub/","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1568300671,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDI3NzI5MTI2Ng=="},"target":"f1eb38912d8c3834cee46b8b249f9c7b148e45ec107127af802f5ba43a0729f5","message":"\u003e datalad install -r https://github.com/gi114/conp-dataset\n\u003e *datalad get \u003cannexed-file\u003e* This generates this error at the get operation:\n\u003e ```\n\u003e [WARNING] Running get resulted in stderr output: git-annex: get: 1 failed [ERROR ] not available\n\u003e Try making some of these repositories available:\n\u003e a45566d0-9023-421d-9707-2732be3b3d84 -- id@host:~/path/to/conp-dataset/projects/FRDR-multimodal\n\u003e\n\u003e (Note that these git remotes have annex-ignore set: origin) \n\u003e [...]  \n\u003e ```\n\u003e\n\u003e So I cannot download the annexed-file content. I searched for the annex-ignore variable mentioned in 'Note that these git remotes have annex-ignore set: origin' and found annex-ignore in my repository in the .git/config and set it to *false* !\n\u003e [...]\n\u003e Is there a way to enforce the variable to be set to false as a default instead of true?\n\nHere is the relevant bit from `man git-annex`:\n\n```\n* `remote.\u003cname\u003e.annex-ignore`\n\n  If set to `true`, prevents git-annex\n  from storing file contents on this remote by default.\n  (You can still request it be used by the `--from` and `--to` options.)\n\n  This is, for example, useful if the remote is located somewhere\n  without git-annex-shell. (For example, if it's on GitHub).\n  Or, it could be used if the network connection between two\n  repositories is too slow to be used normally.\n\n  This does not prevent git-annex sync (or the git-annex assistant) from\n  syncing the git repository to the remote.\n```\n\nThis should give you a pretty good idea of what's going on.  GitHub (\"origin\" here) doesn't support storing annex data and doesn't have git-annex-shell available.  git-annex detects this and sets `.annex-ignore` to \"true\".  You can manually unset it, but it will detect again that it can't work with it and set it again to \"true\".\n\nYou can store your Git repository on GitHub, but you need to expose the content of annexed files through some other remote.\n\nHere's some more information:\n\n  * https://git-annex.branchable.com/tips/centralized_git_repository_tutorial/on_GitHub/\n  * https://www.datalad.org/for/data-publication","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"5f28eb6cf9dc13eefe95e9c4af8c2adace24728a"},"timestamp":1568305150,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzMDg5OTk5Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3632#issuecomment-530899992"},"message":"Hi,\n\nThanks, I see. So I guess that files available on the web that get added\nwith *git annex addurl *do not present the same issue and get downloaded\nwith *datalad get*, am I correct?\n\nSo here it comes an interesting thing that I discussed with Jean Baptiste\nPoline who is my boss here. So the Federated Research Data Repository\n(FRDR)[https://www.frdr.ca/repo/] is the repository currently holding the\ndataset I am working on. In fact, I downloaded it from FRDR via a Globus\naccount. Information on Globus here: https://www.globus.org/\nGlobus is a platform to transfer and share data with collaborators of other\ninstitutions, so it is a way to access datasets just like the one on FRDR\n\nWould it be possible for us to make Globus a new special remote? That would\nbe ideal for my case as well.\n\nI can add a git annex issue on this and, possibly, on creating new special\nremotes on a given need?\n\nLooking forward to hearing back\n\nGiulia\n\n\n\nIl giorno gio 12 set 2019 alle ore 11:02 Kyle Meyer \u003c\nnotifications@github.com\u003e ha scritto:\n\n\u003e datalad install -r https://github.com/gi114/conp-dataset\n\u003e *datalad get * This generates this error at the get operation:\n\u003e\n\u003e [WARNING] Running get resulted in stderr output: git-annex: get: 1 failed [ERROR ] not available\n\u003e Try making some of these repositories available:\n\u003e a45566d0-9023-421d-9707-2732be3b3d84 -- id@host:~/path/to/conp-dataset/projects/FRDR-multimodal\n\u003e\n\u003e (Note that these git remotes have annex-ignore set: origin)\n\u003e [...]\n\u003e\n\u003e So I cannot download the annexed-file content. I searched for the\n\u003e annex-ignore variable mentioned in 'Note that these git remotes have\n\u003e annex-ignore set: origin' and found annex-ignore in my repository in the\n\u003e .git/config and set it to *false* !\n\u003e [...]\n\u003e Is there a way to enforce the variable to be set to false as a default\n\u003e instead of true?\n\u003e\n\u003e Here is the relevant bit from man git-annex:\n\u003e\n\u003e * `remote.\u003cname\u003e.annex-ignore`\n\u003e\n\u003e   If set to `true`, prevents git-annex\n\u003e   from storing file contents on this remote by default.\n\u003e   (You can still request it be used by the `--from` and `--to` options.)\n\u003e\n\u003e   This is, for example, useful if the remote is located somewhere\n\u003e   without git-annex-shell. (For example, if it's on GitHub).\n\u003e   Or, it could be used if the network connection between two\n\u003e   repositories is too slow to be used normally.\n\u003e\n\u003e   This does not prevent git-annex sync (or the git-annex assistant) from\n\u003e   syncing the git repository to the remote.\n\u003e\n\u003e This should give you a pretty good idea of what's going on. GitHub\n\u003e (\"origin\" here) doesn't support storing annex data and doesn't have\n\u003e git-annex-shell available. git-annex detects this and sets .annex-ignore\n\u003e to \"true\". You can manually unset it, but it will detect again that it\n\u003e can't work with it and set it again to \"true\".\n\u003e\n\u003e You can store your Git repository on GitHub, but you need to expose the\n\u003e content of annexed files through some other remote.\n\u003e\n\u003e Here's some more information:\n\u003e\n\u003e    -\n\u003e    https://git-annex.branchable.com/tips/centralized_git_repository_tutorial/on_GitHub/\n\u003e\n\u003e —\n\u003e You are receiving this because you authored the thread.\n\u003e Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3632?email_source=notifications\u0026email_token=AEGS2Z2GJ3UDJHO6YKE27BLQJJKXXA5CNFSM4ISQ2KZ2YY3PNVWWK3TUL52HS4DFVREXG43VMVBW63LNMVXHJKTDN5WW2ZLOORPWSZGOD6SGL6I#issuecomment-530867705\u003e,\n\u003e or mute the thread\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AEGS2Z5HMXSIF4DC5TANGL3QJJKXXANCNFSM4ISQ2KZQ\u003e\n\u003e .\n\u003e","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1568310646,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzMDkzNDEzOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3632#issuecomment-530934138"},"message":"\u003e Thanks, I see. So I guess that files available on the web that get added with *git annex addurl *do not present the same issue and get downloaded with *datalad get*, am I correct?\n\nThat's correct.  git-annex considers [the web as a special remote][0].\n\n[0]: https://git-annex.branchable.com/tips/using_the_web_as_a_special_remote/\n\n\u003e So here it comes an interesting thing that I discussed with Jean Baptiste Poline who is my boss here.\n\nOh, I'm sorry to hear that.  I can only imagine that @jbpoline rules with an iron fist.\n\n\u003e So the Federated Research Data Repository (FRDR)[https://www.frdr.ca/repo/] is the repository currently holding the dataset I am working on. [...] Would it be possible for us to make Globus a new special remote?\n\nYeah, a special remote would be the way to do it.\n\nDepending on the details, you may be able add it as a datalad provider and then datalad's special remote could take care of the download/authentication for you.  (Others will hopefully correct me if I'm wrong here.)  I'm failing to find any documentation on this, but `datalad/downloaders/configs/` directory provides some examples.\n\n\u003e That would be ideal for my case as well. I can add a git annex issue on this and, possibly, on creating new special remotes on a given need?\n\nI'm not sure  this is the sort of special remote that would be added to git-annex proper, but here's [information on how to implement][1] a special remote and here's [an example][2] of a special remote that is currently being developed.  I do find [one mention of globus][3] on git-annex's site, posted by @yarikoptic.  Much of that discussion is focused on an unrelated aspect (reuse of a UUID), but it seems there was at least the idea of a globus special remote a few months ago.\n\n[ This thread has become pretty long and is unrelated to the original issue.  Please open a dedicated issue if you have further follow-up questions.  This will prevent the discussion from getting buried in this issue and make it more likely to receive attention/feedback from others. ]\n\n[1]: https://git-annex.branchable.com/design/external_special_remote_protocol/\n[2]: https://github.com/datalad/git-annex-ria-remote\n[3]: https://git-annex.branchable.com/todo/globus_special_remote_as_a___34__transport__34___layer/","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"5f28eb6cf9dc13eefe95e9c4af8c2adace24728a"},"timestamp":1568320149,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzMDk5MzI1NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3632#issuecomment-530993254"},"message":"Hi,\n\nThank you again for your message - I will discuss with JB what option to\nchoose. I will open a dedicated issue if necessary and will keep you up to\ndate.\n\nBest\nGiulia\n\nIl giorno gio 12 set 2019 alle ore 13:50 Kyle Meyer \u003c\nnotifications@github.com\u003e ha scritto:\n\n\u003e Thanks, I see. So I guess that files available on the web that get added\n\u003e with *git annex addurl *do not present the same issue and get downloaded\n\u003e with *datalad get*, am I correct?\n\u003e\n\u003e That's correct. git-annex considers the web as a special remote\n\u003e \u003chttps://git-annex.branchable.com/tips/using_the_web_as_a_special_remote/\u003e\n\u003e .\n\u003e\n\u003e So here it comes an interesting thing that I discussed with Jean Baptiste\n\u003e Poline who is my boss here.\n\u003e\n\u003e Oh, I'm sorry to hear that. I can only imagine that @jbpoline\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/jbpoline\u003e rules with an iron fist.\n\u003e\n\u003e So the Federated Research Data Repository (FRDR)[https://www.frdr.ca/repo/]\n\u003e is the repository currently holding the dataset I am working on. [...]\n\u003e Would it be possible for us to make Globus a new special remote?\n\u003e\n\u003e Yeah, a special remote would be the way to do it.\n\u003e\n\u003e Depending on the details, you may be able add it as a datalad provider and\n\u003e then datalad's special remote could take care of the\n\u003e download/authentication for you. (Others will hopefully correct me if I'm\n\u003e wrong here.) I'm failing to find any documentation on this, but\n\u003e datalad/downloaders/configs/ directory provides some examples.\n\u003e\n\u003e That would be ideal for my case as well. I can add a git annex issue on\n\u003e this and, possibly, on creating new special remotes on a given need?\n\u003e\n\u003e I'm not sure this is the sort of special remote that would be added to\n\u003e git-annex proper, but here's information on how to implement\n\u003e \u003chttps://git-annex.branchable.com/design/external_special_remote_protocol/\u003e\n\u003e a special remote and here's an example\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/datalad/git-annex-ria-remote\u003e of a special remote\n\u003e that is currently being developed. I do find one mention of globus\n\u003e \u003chttps://git-annex.branchable.com/todo/globus_special_remote_as_a___34__transport__34___layer/\u003e\n\u003e on git-annex's site, posted by @yarikoptic \u003chttps://github.com/yarikoptic\u003e.\n\u003e Much of that discussion is focused on an unrelated aspect (reuse of a\n\u003e UUID), but it seems there was at least the idea of a globus special remote\n\u003e a few months ago.\n\u003e\n\u003e [ This thread has become pretty long and is unrelated to the original\n\u003e issue. Please open a dedicated issue if you have further follow-up\n\u003e questions. This will prevent the discussion from getting buried in this\n\u003e issue and make it more likely to receive attention/feedback from others. ]\n\u003e\n\u003e —\n\u003e You are receiving this because you authored the thread.\n\u003e Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3632?email_source=notifications\u0026email_token=AEGS2Z63H4JF5RA35M3ZURLQJJ6PRA5CNFSM4ISQ2KZ2YY3PNVWWK3TUL52HS4DFVREXG43VMVBW63LNMVXHJKTDN5WW2ZLOORPWSZGOD6SWS6Q#issuecomment-530934138\u003e,\n\u003e or mute the thread\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AEGS2Z6WOIWQEYEVKK7XPE3QJJ6PRANCNFSM4ISQ2KZQ\u003e\n\u003e .\n\u003e","files":null}]}